dragons cast a large shadow over the  mythologies of the world  few legendary monsters can compare to  these gigantic beasts  but what if dragons cast a slightly  smaller shadow what if some dragons  weren't huge monsters but strange and  secretive creatures evolved to live  hidden lives in the shadows and margins  of the world the amazing artist and  writer andy frazier imagines a world  where dragons are all around us from the  highest mountains to the deepest forest  concentrated primarily in the author's  homeland of wales these smaller dragons  or micro dragons as i call them make up  for their reduced size with their  incredible diversity and ingenious  survival strategies the author has  released many field guides on these tiny  wonders that explore their evolution  anatomy behavior and habitats in great  detail which you can find linked below  so for this entry into the archive i'll  assume the role of naturalist and  observe some of these creatures out in  the field  now let's head off into the welsh  countryside and discover the secret  biology of micro dragons  at the start of the expedition the first  place i look for micro dragons is on the  low branches of trees here a  draconotophthalmus suns themselves in  the morning light while this stunning  specimen can fly like a bird internally  they have several key distinctions that  make their anatomy unique  if we examined their skeleton we'd find  dragon bones are denser than a birds  making them better suited to life on the  ground where most dragon species spend  part of their time  in many species the neural spines along  the backbone are also greatly enlarged  forming obvious spikes  in the case of  draconotophthalmus these long spikes can  be raised and lowered as a threat  display for the notoph thalamus survives  by trying to appear as dangerous and  intimidating as possible making loud  vocalizations and threatening postures  as i pass by from an evolutionary  perspective this approach has been  remarkably successful for a dragon that  in reality is a very timid herbivore  venturing deeper into the woodland i  find that no two dragons have the same  survival strategy  splashing along the surface of a stream  is a brightly colored emerald scythe  unlike the notoph thalamus this more  basal species is covered in fine wind  resistant fibers the prehistoric  appearance of the emerald scythe sheds  light into the origins of micro dragons  the distant ancestor of welsh dragons  the dimorphodon macronyx branched off  from pterosaurs some 190 million years  ago surviving the mass extinction that  wiped out their more famous cousins  thanks to their diminutive size and more  adaptable nature today modern dragons  have branched off into ever more  specialized subspecies with the emerald  scythe the scavenger of fallen seeds and  berries they also have a fondness for  playing among the spray of rivers  although there appears to be no  practical benefit for this and is  probably done just for fun  near a patch of wildflowers a barely  audible buzzing draws me closer  hovering near a carefully chosen flower  is the fascinating draco sep diana on  delicate green and red wings draco  septiana darts between flowers feeding  on nectar with the aid of their long  thin tongue this behavior closely  mirrors that of hummingbirds which live  a similar high metabolism lifestyle  feeding on flower nectar it might be  surprising that such disparate species  would converge on the same strategy but  there's evidence to support such an  outcome hummingbird hawk moths are  pollinating insects that independently  became so similar to real hummingbirds  that the two are often mixed up and like  all species occupying the hummingbird  niche the draco sep diana performs a  vital role within their local ecosystem  transporting pollen on the long red  filaments that trail from their head as  they move from flower to flower  pollinating plants is one thing but  among the branches of holly trees i find  one unusual specialist has taken their  connection with the foliage to new  extremes  draco ilexis is a species whose wings  and body have evolved to resemble the  leaves of holly trees making them appear  like living plants as strange as this  specimen might seem there's a long  precedent for such camouflage in nature  defensive mimicry is relatively common  in the larger animal kingdom as  mimicking a plant is a good way to get a  carnivore to ignore you as a food source  if a draco ilexis is spotted however  they have one last offense like the  trees they mimic their wings are covered  in tough thorn-like spikes which make  draco alexis more trouble than they're  worth to most predators  within the foliage of fruit trees i  discover another dragon species makes  clever use of plant mimicry the rotund  draco acidium looks like a round fruit  themselves although they may appear  dangerous with their protruding fangs  this dragon's large teeth are only used  to pierce the tough outer peels of the  fruits they feed on for the draco  acidium is a frugivore meaning their  diet consists mainly of raw fruits many  animals engage in frugivorous behavior  and while this may seem like pilfering  many fruit producing plants benefit from  such interaction certain frugivores  actually assist plants by dispersing  their seeds after digesting them a type  of interaction known as mutualism which  side of the fence the draco acidium  falls on is unknown but it's certainly  possible these tiny dragons act as  natural gardeners of the fruit groves  they call home being slow and flightless  draco essidium also relies on these  trees to keep them hidden although as a  last resort they also possess a powerful  acidic bite  yet other dragons use camouflage for a  more sinister purpose  draco muscular possesses one of the most  unusual hunting strategies of any dragon  able to remain absolutely motionless for  hours at a time they hold their wings  over their head and release a pungent  odor to attract potential prey  carnivorous plants like venus flytraps  have a similar strategy using sweet  smelling nectar to lure in unlucky  insects and like a fly trap once the  draco moves kepler's prey is within  reach the dragon clasps their  specialized wings together stunning  their prey before dropping them into the  waiting jaws beneath for this dragon  patience is more than a virtue  yet not all dragons have evolved to  blend in although just a few centimeters  long the vivid coloration of species  like draco favilla mean they can be  easily spotted and that's exactly the  point like the glaring colors of the  poison dart frog the draco favilla's  coloration is aposomatic meaning it  warns predators the species isn't worth  attacking and while the poison dart frog  backs up their aposomatic hue with  poison draco favilla's color warns of  their deadly venom delivered by tiny  sharp fang jaws this dragon's venom is  potent enough to incapacitate creatures  many times larger than themselves i'll  need to watch where i step  and hidden among the blades of grass  some dragons are even smaller  just two centimeters in length draco  coccinos is perhaps the smallest species  of dragon on the planet indeed they are  so small they stretch our understanding  of what a dragon can be yet remarkably  there is some precedent for a reptile  this size  nano chameleons are chameleon subspecies  that might be the smallest reptiles on  earth at roughly the size of a sunflower  seed at sizes like this the world can be  a daunting place to navigate luckily the  draco coccinos possesses two hair-like  antennae packed with highly sensitive  sense receptors that help them detect  potential threats over long distances  and so this insect-sized dragon does  quite well for themselves within their  unusual niche  in other regions some dragons grow  larger around the curve of a woodland  stream i happen upon a scarcely seen  specimen a draco called daute glistens  in the afternoon sun body poised for a  quick dive into the water the caudate is  an amphibious hunter with webbed toes  and a rudder-like tail the unusually  long and narrow snout of this dragon  contains numerous recurved teeth for  latching on to fish  occupying a niche similar to storks or  herons the draco caudate are natural  fishermen they are also experts at  stealthy movement with their silent  nature making them difficult to track  i'm lucky to be able to observe one  upon the rocky coast some dragons have  become even more specialized aquatic  lifeforms perched on a rock in the  afternoon sun is the bizarre crimson  kimuk possessing a lobster-like tail  fluke for paddling under the frothy  waves this organism fills a niche one  might not expect a dragon to occupy the  crimson kimmu hunts in shallow coastal  waters feeding on mollusks and small  fish behaving like seals or sea lions  they spend a good amount of time out of  the water surveying their territory or  just lazing about upon the rocks  on the front of their snouts they  possess curious tentacular appendages  that further separate their appearance  from the dragons of fairy tales how such  a feature first evolved is a biological  mystery i wonder if i'll find other  species with a similar adaptation  below the surface of the freezing waters  some specimen have given up on land  almost entirely the north nofire are  sleek agile aquatic hunters using their  powerful wings and broad tail for  propulsion their soft downy feathers  help keep them warm in the cold currents  feathers on an aquatic animal might seem  strange but penguins possess feathers  that are shorter and stiffer than those  of most birds keeping them streamlined  in the water we can guess that the no  fear have taken a page from the  penguin's book when it comes to their  feather structure like penguins the  north nofwere are ungainly when not in  the water only able to waddle about in  their elements however these  torpedo-like swimmers are something to  behold  further up the coast seabirds flap above  the crashing waves  and hidden among the flock fearless  surrender storms dive close to the  raging tide these exceptionally rare  dragons can be seen flying high even in  the stormiest weather with such apparent  boldness it would be easy to  sentimentalize these dragons as  courageous yet like seagulls cerano  storms are perhaps closer to pests than  they are to adventurers and spend much  of their time scavenging the picnic  leftovers of tourists while valiantly  stealing discarded food serene storms  often release a call that has been  described as a chuckling sound  considering their surprisingly  intelligent nature it is tempting to  think that these little nuisances are  laughing at us  heading inland i notice that high above  the treetops a flock soars through the  open skies  the pale blue draco ariel lingi are at  first difficult to spot against the  afternoon sky but their graceful curved  wings and v-shaped tail give them an  unmistakable silhouette these graceful  creatures are aerial acrobats spending  much of their time above the canopy but  are able to swoop among the branches in  pursuit of insects certain woodland  raptors are able to perform similar  feats of agility pursuing prey at top  speeds through dense forests without  hitting trees but the draco ariel lingi  can best display their swiftness in the  open air where great flocks such as this  one bank and dive for no reason beyond  their personal enjoyment  soon the night will come to the forest  in the fading light i find hidden among  the trunks a primeval dragon that is  endured into the modern day draco  borealis are a species with a downright  prehistoric appearance resembling  pterosaurs more than any other dragons  i've encountered perhaps the most  distinguishing feature of this species  is their colorful throat pouch iguanas  have similar structures under their  necks that help with thermoregulation  yet the pouch of draco borealis has a  far stranger function an air reserve  this dragon occasionally dives into  lakes to catch fish and uses their  bizarre pouch to extend their lung  capacity the extremes of dragon survival  strategies cannot be underestimated  with the sun about to set on the far  side of the valley i come across a  particularly old region of the woodland  here a heavily built brute with long  curved horns patrols the rotting  branches this is draco baparousas and  they have come here for a meal but it's  not the sort of meal one might expect of  such a beast  dracobabarusis is an insectivore with  their diet composed primarily of ants  and termites they use their highly  specialized head to dig through rotten  bark and leaf litter in search of their  crawling food their top horns also serve  as defensive structures that help draco  barbarus maintain exclusive rights to  their side of the rotting logs with the  sun now almost gone i'll leave  dracobaprusis to their supper  in time dusk falls upon the forest in  the low light i find even more alien  looking dragon species become active  lighting up the dark are the glowing  eyes and tails of draco lampyridae the  function of this natural bioluminescence  isn't immediately apparent but we can  find some potential clues in the natural  world the biochemical glow of fireflies  is thought to serve a number of  functions from scaring off predators to  attracting mates and since these draco  lampyridae are in a pair i wonder if the  glow they give off is perhaps a method  of communication if this is indeed the  case i don't want to interrupt what  seems to be a private conversation  amongst the ancient and decaying trees  of the darkest most inaccessible depths  of the forest a poorly understood  creature is lurking draco johanssi is  scarcely recognizable as a dragon at a  glance possessing a mass of highly  dexterous tentacles surrounding their  mouth while i've spotted other species  with similar appendages draco johansi  takes this adaptation to a new level  like the snout of a star-nosed mole  these appendages help the species  navigate their environment through  tactile input as the draco johansi are  partially sightless yet unlike the star  nose mole this dragon also uses their  appendages to ensnare prey and while  details of diet and behavior remain  largely unknown signs of aggression tell  me to keep my distance  in the dark woods i've seen dreamlike  specimens and beasts that seem to have  wandered from a nightmare yet the last  dragon i encounter is both at once draco  ignis ferendi is an enigmatic omnivore  with a striking appearance they appear  to be bioluminescent with a glowing  green liquid dripping from their teeth  yet draco ignis ferendi's light is  actually borrowed from their primary  food source  glowworms these tiny larva give off a  subtle green glimmer and when mixed with  draco ignis ferendi saliva the glimmer  becomes a hypnotic light in the dark  forest like most species i've glimpsed  today draco ignis ferendi doesn't fit  the traditional myth of the dragon yet  they are undoubtedly a mesmerizing  lifeform  the dragons we've seen today may be  smaller than those of myth and legend  but thanks to their incredible survival  strategies they are no less majestic and  there's so many more species and  biological details to discover in the  incredible volumes of andy frazier there  are even rumors of older dragons that  blend into the moss and stone and that  grow to colossal sizes which the author  will explore in a new book out later  this summer if you find these creatures  as fascinating as i do please follow and  support the artist using the links below  and consider checking out these books  for yourself  and as always thanks for watching  if you enjoyed this entry please lend  your support and like subscribe and hit  the notification icon to stay up to date  on all things curious  see you in the next video  you

